\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The 2025--2026 workload shifts seen through \cref  {eq:model}. (a)\nobreakspace  {}Bytes read per decode token for the weights (W4) and the KV cache as context grows. Dots mark where the cache read equals the weight read: 18k tokens at FP16, 37k at INT8, and 65k with an MLA-style latent cache. A 2-bit cache and a hybrid model with one attention layer in four coincide. (b)\nobreakspace  {}Time per generated token on the phone-class NPU, split by binding term. Reasoning uses 32k context. Speculative decoding drafts four tokens with a draft model one-tenth the size and 70\% acceptance. Diffusion uses blocks of 32 tokens and 8 denoising steps. The MoE row is a 21B-total, 3.6B-active model of the gpt-oss-20b class\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {openai2025gptoss}; its flash variant assumes 10\% of expert reads miss DRAM and are served from 4\,GB/s flash. (c)\nobreakspace  {}Non-GEMM share and time per token on the weight-resident configuration for the same dense-model scenarios. The ``pre-RoPE keys'' scenario stores keys before rotation, so every cached key is rotated again at every step.}}{27}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:frontier}{{13}{27}{The 2025--2026 workload shifts seen through \cref {eq:model}. (a)~Bytes read per decode token for the weights (W4) and the KV cache as context grows. Dots mark where the cache read equals the weight read: 18k tokens at FP16, 37k at INT8, and 65k with an MLA-style latent cache. A 2-bit cache and a hybrid model with one attention layer in four coincide. (b)~Time per generated token on the phone-class NPU, split by binding term. Reasoning uses 32k context. Speculative decoding drafts four tokens with a draft model one-tenth the size and 70\% acceptance. Diffusion uses blocks of 32 tokens and 8 denoising steps. The MoE row is a 21B-total, 3.6B-active model of the gpt-oss-20b class~\cite {openai2025gptoss}; its flash variant assumes 10\% of expert reads miss DRAM and are served from 4\,GB/s flash. (c)~Non-GEMM share and time per token on the weight-resident configuration for the same dense-model scenarios. The ``pre-RoPE keys'' scenario stores keys before rotation, so every cached key is rotated again at every step}{figure.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:frontier@cref}{{[figure][13][]13}{[1][26][]27}}
